// Syllabus — Thomas W. Judson, Abstract Algebra: Theory and Applications, 2023 edition (GNU FDL). Book-anchored: numbers are Judson's.
//
// SOURCE. The book's site (http://abstract.pugetsound.edu, with the PDFs listed at /download.html, e.g. aata-20230803.pdf)
// could not be reached from this environment (DNS failure; https://abstract.ups.edu also refused), so the inventory was
// extracted from the book's own PreTeXt source, https://github.com/twjudson/aata, at commit 30ad145 ("date for 2023
// edition", 10 Aug 2023) — the source of the 2023 annual edition named in collection.js. The numbers were not counted by
// hand: the PreTeXt 2.55 stylesheets (pretext-html.xsl, with the book's publisher/public.xml: divisions level 2, blocks
// level 1) were run over that source and each block's number was read back, so they are the numbers printed in the HTML
// and PDF editions. An independent re-count with a small script agreed on all 597 numbered blocks, and the current master
// (Sept 2026) has the same block structure, so the numbers also hold for the online edition at /aata/.
// Section refs ('Judson §x.y') name the section that contains the item. The chapter list was checked against src/aata.xml
// (23 chapters, Preliminaries to Galois Theory).
//
// NUMBERING. Judson numbers everything in a chapter with ONE counter: theorems, propositions, lemmas, corollaries,
// principles, remarks, examples, figures and tables (a figure inside an example takes its own number). So the numbers
// below have large gaps, nearly all of them examples and figures. Judson NEVER numbers definitions — they are bold terms
// in running text. The load-bearing ones are listed with the number of the nearest PRECEDING numbered block (example,
// figure or result) in the same chapter plus a letter: 3.7a = group (defined just after Figure 3.7), 3.23a = subgroup,
// 16.22a = ideal. A definition that comes before the first numbered block of its chapter takes that block's number plus a
// letter (6.1a = cosets, 7.1a = cryptosystems, 10.1a = normal subgroups). Attaching a definition to the block before it,
// rather than after it, keeps every definition in front of the results that use it, so deps point backwards in the
// numbering (the same convention as prob). Five results that the text proves without numbering are lettered the same way:
// the class equation (14.11b), RSA message verification (7.5a), and the impossibility of doubling the cube, squaring the
// circle and trisecting an angle (21.44a–c); 7.4a (public-key cryptography) is an unnumbered concept from §7.2.
//
// WHAT IS LISTED (427 items). Every numbered result of the book — 278: Theorem 136, Proposition 55, Lemma 44, Corollary
// 39, the three Principles of §2.1 and Judson's "Axiom 19.12, Principle of Duality" (kind Principle) — each with Judson's
// own label as its kind; 109 lettered definitions; the 5 lettered unnumbered results above and the lettered concept 7.4a;
// and 34 of the 245 numbered examples, those that introduce a famous object, counterexample or algorithm (2.12 Euclidean
// algorithm, 3.15 quaternion group, 4.28 repeated squaring, 7.5 RSA, 16.7 Hamilton's quaternions, 18.10 a non-UFD, 23.25
// the Galois group of x⁴ − 2, 23.33 an unsolvable quintic, …), under the example's printed number with kind Concept or
// Algorithm.
// OMITTED: the other 211 examples, all 54 figures and 11 tables, the 9 numbered remarks (4.4, 5.3, 5.11, 6.5, 6.14, 14.8,
// 16.28, 18.33, 21.16 — asides on notation or converses; 6.14's point is carried by Proposition 6.15), exercises, Sage
// sections, historical notes and §1.1 (vocabulary of proofs, nothing numbered).
//
// CROSS-LINKS. The book proves many results that other collections also hold (Euclid, Bézout, Fermat, Euler and the CRT
// in nt; ciphers and RSA in crypto; Hamming distance, linear and BCH codes in info; vector spaces in ladr; Burnside in comb
// and rep; wallpaper groups in art and xtal; the fundamental theorem of algebra in pma and rca). Since this collection is
// book-anchored, Judson's numbered results are kept and linked to those items by `see`; `deps` is used only where the
// explanation genuinely leans on the other collection (comb-1.6 binomial coefficients, ladr-9.49/9.50 determinants,
// logic-11.4 Zorn's lemma for algebraic closures, nt-8.12 transcendence of π). Nothing here depends on rep (rep builds on
// groups), and rep appears only in `see`.
